## 15 August 2026 (cont. 7)

### Objective
Build and validate the discrete tick-jump model: X continuous (Brownian),
M moves only in +-delta ticks via two competing jump intensities that lean
toward X, no spread/parity mechanism yet (fixed spread deferred). Isolate
what changes merely because the mid jumps, before touching trading.

### Question
Does src/simulate_discrete.py satisfy every item on the validation
checklist: M tick-only, X continuous, up/down jump asymmetry matching the
sign of G, mean(G)~0, and Cov(G_t,G_t+h) decaying like s_G^2 * e^(-alpha h)?

### Model
New file src/simulate_discrete.py. Per step: move X by
sigma_X*sqrt(dt)*Z; compute g=M-X; lambda_up=mu+(alpha/delta)*max(-g,0),
lambda_down=mu+(alpha/delta)*max(g,0); p_up=lambda_up*dt,
p_down=lambda_down*dt; single uniform draw picks up-jump / down-jump / no
jump; save G=M-X post-jump. Parameters: alpha=0.5 (reused from the OU
section for comparability), mu=1.0, delta=0.005, sigma_X=0.02, dt=0.001
(chosen so max observed (lambda_up+lambda_down)*dt = 0.0085, comfortably
<<1, satisfying the one-event-per-step approximation the assignment
requires).

### Expected result
All six checklist items pass; in particular the gap's autocorrelation
should track e^(-alpha h) with alpha=0.5 closely once enough data is used.

### Actual result
Structural checks (100s path, dt=0.001) all passed cleanly:
- mid moves only in +-1 tick steps: True
- efficient price continuous: std(dX)=0.000634 vs theory sigma_X*sqrt(dt)=0.000632
- P(up-jump | G<0)=0.00295 vs P(up-jump | G>=0)=0.00101 (up-jumps ~3x more
  likely when G<0, as expected)
- P(down-jump | G>0)=0.00274 vs P(down-jump | G<=0)=0.00075 (same asymmetry,
  mirrored)
- mean(G) = -0.00125 (near zero, single-path noise)
- std(G) = 0.02070 (close to the OU section's s_G=0.02, though not forced
  to match exactly -- delta, mu were picked freely, not calibrated to hit
  0.02 precisely)

Autocovariance decay -- see "Problems" below for the full story, but the
short version: a 100-2000s path gave a fitted decay rate of 0.68-0.97
against an input alpha=0.5, which looked like a real bug or a genuine
model discrepancy. It was neither. A 6000s run (50s burn-in discarded)
gives fitted rate = 0.5382, and independent cross-validation (below)
confirms alpha=0.5 is correct. Plots: paper/notes/discrete_path.png (mid
vs X, and G, over a 10s window -- visibly tick-stepped M chasing continuous
X) and paper/notes/discrete_autocov.png (empirical autocorrelation vs
e^(-0.5h), now a near-perfect match out to lag 6s).

### Interpretation
The model is correct; the ORIGINAL autocovariance-based validation was not
a reliable way to check it at the durations first tried. Diagnosis, in
order of what was tried:
1. Single 100s path: fitted rate 0.9674, way off.
2. Averaged over 20 independent 100s paths: 0.6688 -- better but still
   clearly wrong, and averaging independent seeds should have killed
   ordinary sampling noise. This ruled out "just one unlucky seed."
3. Single 2000s path, burn-in removed, both step orderings (evaluate
   intensity before vs after moving X within a step) tested: 0.64-0.68 in
   both cases -- ruled out the within-step look-ahead ordering as the
   cause (it was a reasonable suspect: the assignment's specified order
   evaluates the jump intensity using G computed AFTER X has already moved
   that step, a look-ahead relative to the true continuous-time
   compensator; this turned out not to be the source of the bias, though
   it remains a real, separate, second-order discretization detail worth
   knowing about).
4. Decisive cross-checks that do NOT rely on a single long time-averaged
   path: (a) propagate 4000 independent replicates from a FIXED G_0=0.05
   for h=0.5/1/2s and check E[G_h]/G_0 against e^(-alpha h) directly --
   matched theory to ~0.1-0.4%, unambiguously confirming alpha=0.5 for the
   conditional mean. (b) Cross-sectional test: burn in 3000 independent
   paths to an approximately-stationary G_t, then continue each by h and
   compute corr(G_t, G_t+h) across the ensemble (not time-averaged within
   one path) -- also matched e^(-alpha h) closely (e.g. h=1: empirical
   0.595 vs theory 0.607). Both of these independently confirm alpha=0.5
   and rule out a model/dynamics bug.
5. Final resolution: reran the ORIGINAL single-long-path, time-averaged
   autocovariance-and-log-linear-fit method (same as step 1-3) but with
   duration=8000s on the SAME seed that gave 0.68 at 2000s -- fitted rate
   came back at 0.5142, matching theory. Duration, not ordering or model
   correctness, was the actual variable. The mechanism: fitting a single
   exponential via OLS on log(autocovariance ratio) across lags out to 6s
   from a time-averaged single path is dominated by the noisiest (longest-
   lag) points, and at 100-2000s there simply aren't enough effectively
   independent long-lag samples (few "escapes" to lag-6s separation
   relative to the ~2s decorrelation time) for that fit to be reliable --
   not a bias in the strict sense (bias vanished with more data, which a
   true bias would not do), but a finite-sample fit that happened to read
   as "wrong" with high apparent confidence at short-to-moderate duration.

The general lesson: a single time-averaged-path ACF check is a weaker
validation tool than it looks, and disagreeing with theory at moderate
sample size is not enough to conclude the model is wrong -- cross-checking
via a completely different estimation method (here: fixed-start
propagation, and cross-sectional ensemble correlation) before concluding
"bug" saved from chasing a phantom discretization-ordering issue.

### Problems or questions
- delta, mu were chosen freely (not calibrated) -- std(G)=0.0207 landed
  close to the OU section's s_G=0.02 by luck/reasonable parameter choice,
  not by design. If a tight match to the OU section's s_G is wanted for
  the upcoming theta_D comparison, calibrate mu (or delta) against the
  measured std(G) rather than assuming it matches.
- The look-ahead-vs-no-look-ahead step ordering was ruled out as the cause
  of the (illusory) large bias, but a small, real, second-order
  discretization effect from that ordering choice was never precisely
  quantified (it was dominated by the much larger estimation-noise effect
  during debugging). Worth a clean, dedicated comparison at fixed high
  duration if the exact ordering ever matters downstream (per the
  assignment's own spec, we use "evaluate intensity after moving X" i.e.
  the look-ahead order, since that is literally what was specified).
- validate_decay_rate defaults to 6000s + 6s of lags; this is noticeably
  slower than the structural checks (a few seconds vs ~15s total) --
  acceptable for a one-off validation run, would need revisiting if this
  needs to run routinely (e.g. inside a Monte Carlo sweep).

### Next step
Run the same threshold sweep used in the OU section on this discrete gap
series (run_trading_rule already works on any gap array + dt, no
modification needed) and compare the discrete model's empirical optimum
against theta_D from the OU section -- this is the comparison the paper's
own "~20% jump-overshoot" finding predicts, and now there is a validated
simulator to actually test it against.

