there (~0.011) are roughly double those at delta=0.005 (~0.0057), and
0.018-vs-theta_D reaches 2.71 SE (the largest deviation seen anywhere in
this sweep). This is not a contradiction -- it's the regime running out:
theta_D and theta* were computed ONCE from s_G=0.02 and held fixed across
the whole sweep (deliberately, to test whether that ORIGINAL
recommendation holds up, not to re-optimize per delta), but at delta=0.02
the tick is as large as the gap's entire stationary dispersion, well
outside the "delta << theta - phi" regime the paper's own approximation
requires (Section 3, "This is where we work: delta << theta-phi"). The
model is being tested past where its own assumptions apply; that theta_emp
=0.018 (selected specifically for delta=0.005) stops looking optimal there
is expected, not a robustness failure of the theta_D-vs-theta* claim.

### Problems or questions
- delta=0.02's larger deviations are a single data point at the edge of
  the model's validity range -- not independently re-tested with more
  seeds, since the point of this sweep was breadth (many settings) over
  depth (high precision at each), consistent with not chasing significance.
- s_G was not recalibrated per delta/dt; a delta-dependent s_G would move
  where "theta_D" and "theta*" themselves sit, which this sweep
  deliberately did not do (it tests robustness of the FIXED
  recommendation, not a re-derived one per setting).

